# ==============================================================================
# DaVinci Resolve hardware — licence dongles and control panels
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# Some people unlock DaVinci Resolve Studio with a small USB stick (a "dongle")
# rather than a licence key, and some people drive it with a physical control
# panel — the desk of coloured trackballs colourists use. Linux does not let an
# ordinary program talk to arbitrary USB hardware unless a rule like this one
# says it may.
#
# Blackmagic's own installer writes exactly these two rules. It cannot help us,
# for two reasons:
#
#   1. On AquariusOS, Resolve is installed INSIDE a container. Rules written in
#      there apply to nothing — the real USB devices belong to the computer, not
#      to the container.
#   2. AquariusOS's system folder is read-only. An installer cannot add files to
#      it after the fact. Anything of this kind has to travel inside the image,
#      which is what this file is.
#
# So the rules ship here, on the host, and they work whether or not Resolve is
# ever installed. A rule for hardware nobody has plugged in does nothing at all.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO VENDOR NUMBERS, AND A NAMING MUDDLE WORTH KNOWING ABOUT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every USB device announces who made it as a four-character number.
#
#   1edb   Blackmagic Design — their control panels, and their capture cards
#   096e   Feitian Technologies — who make the USB licence dongles Blackmagic
#          ships with Resolve Studio
#
# Blackmagic's installer writes one number into a file called
# 75-davincipanel.rules and the other into 75-sdx.rules, and different copies of
# their installer disagree about which goes where — published copies of those
# two files can be found with the numbers the other way round.
#
# It makes no practical difference: both rules do the same thing, so having both
# numbers is what matters and which file they live in does not. We ship one file
# with both, labelled by what the number actually is, so nobody has to work this
# out again.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY MODE 0666
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0666 means "any program run by any user on this computer may talk to this
# device". That is broader than we would normally choose, and it is what
# Blackmagic's own installer sets. We match them deliberately: this is hardware
# nobody on the team owns, so a rule that is known to work is worth more than a
# tighter rule that has never been tested against a real dongle. It applies only
# to devices from these two manufacturers — plugging in anything else is
# unaffected.
#
# uaccess is added alongside it. That is the modern way: it hands the device to
# whoever is physically logged in at the screen. Belt and braces, so that if a
# future AquariusOS tightens the 0666 line, the dongle keeps working.
#
# If you own a panel or a dongle and it is not seen, the first thing to check is
# whether it is one of these two vendors:
#     lsusb
# ==============================================================================

# Blackmagic Design's own hardware — DaVinci control panels, capture cards.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1edb", MODE="0666", TAG+="uaccess"

# Feitian — the USB licence dongle that unlocks DaVinci Resolve Studio.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTRS{idVendor}=="096e", MODE="0666", TAG+="uaccess"
